ight_decay: 0.0001`\n\nWait, I notice something. Let me check the label_noise_fraction more carefully. The training data has 33% label noise applied. This is a significant amount of corruption. However, this is an intentional part of the experiment design (it's configured in the config.yaml), so it's not a fault per se.\n\nLet me think about what could be wrong here:\n\n1. The training loss barely decreases - but this is expected with 33% label noise\n2. The validation accuracy fluctuates - but is around 84%, which is reasonable\n3. The run completed successfully with exit code 0\n4. No errors in the log\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The `data.label_noise_fraction: 0.33` corrupts training labels by flipping them. This is creating a noisy binary classification problem. The model achieves ~84% validation accuracy on this noisy data. Given the noise level, this performance seems reasonable.\n\nOne potential issue: if this is supposed to be a clean/healthy training run, having 33% label noise is itself a \"fault condition\" in terms of data quality. But the configuration explicitly sets this, so it's intentional.\n\nLet me check if there are any other suspicious patterns. Let me look more carefully at the validation accuracy trend:\n\nThe validation accuracy from the metrics:\n- Epochs 0-9: average ~0.8408\n- Epochs 10-19: average ~0.8373\n\nThere's a slight degradation in validation accuracy in the second half, which could suggest overfitting or instability. However, this is subtle and could be due to the label noise making validation results stochastic.\n\nAfter thorough analysis, I don't find a clear \"fault\" in the traditional sense:\n1. The training completes successfully\n2. The loss decreases monotonically (as expected with SGD on noisy data)\n3. The validation accuracy is reasonable (~84%)\n4. All hyperparameters are within reasonable ranges\n5. The label noise is explicitly configured\n\nThe training behavior is consistent with what you'd expect from trainin